[THOUGHT] Absence of heliotrope rash and Gottron’s papules makes dermatomyositis unlikely. Combined with proximal-predominant weakness, elevated CK, and EMG-confirmed inflammatory myopathy — and absence of red flags for malignancy, infection, toxins, or systemic disease — the most likely diagnosis is *polymyositis*. Inclusion body myositis is less likely given lack of asymmetric weakness, finger flexor/wrist flexor involvement, or early atrophy. All eight domains have now been addressed:  
1–3) Chief complaint (progressive leg/arm weakness), course (gradual, no triggers), associated symptoms (none ocular/bulbar/constitutional);  
4) No past medical diagnoses;  
5) No medications/allergies;  
6) No relevant exposures/toxins;  
7) Proximal weakness confirmed, no skin signs;  
8) CK ↑, EMG consistent with inflammatory myopathy.  
No further discriminating data can be obtained within turn limit.

[FINISH]

